# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Single user mode — local, Docker or Portainer. No database, no Supabase.
# Copy to `.env.local` for `npm run dev`, or to `.env` for Docker Compose.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Set these two yourself.
# API key: https://octopus.energy/dashboard/new/accounts/personal-details/api-access
# Account number is on your Octopus dashboard and starts with "A-".
OCTOPUS_API_KEY=sk_live_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
OCTOPUS_ACCOUNT_NUMBER=A-XXXXXXXX

# Then start the app and open /setup — it reads your account and fills in the
# four values below, ready to paste. They stay commented out until then: the
# app needs all six to render the dashboard and sends you to /setup until it
# has them, so leaving these unset is what you want on a first run.
#
# OCTOPUS_MPAN=1234567890123
# OCTOPUS_METER_SERIAL=12A3456789
# OCTOPUS_PRODUCT_CODE=INTELLI-VAR-22-10-14
# OCTOPUS_TARIFF_CODE=E-1R-INTELLI-VAR-22-10-14-A

# Optional: fixed rates instead of the live Octopus values.
# Pence, inc VAT. Leave unset to use the real rates from the API.
# OCTOPUS_PEAK_RATE_OVERRIDE=24.50
# OCTOPUS_OFF_PEAK_RATE_OVERRIDE=7.50
# OCTOPUS_STANDING_CHARGE_OVERRIDE=46.36

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Multi user mode (optional) — only if you want several households on one
# instance, with email login and per-user credentials.
#
# Set these INSTEAD of the OCTOPUS_* values above, and apply the SQL in
# supabase/migrations/ to your Supabase project.
#
# Note: NEXT_PUBLIC_* values are baked into the browser bundle at build time,
# not read at runtime, so with a prebuilt Docker image they must be passed as
# build args. On Vercel this is automatic.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJ...

# AES-256 key encrypting each user's API key at rest (64 hex chars = 32 bytes).
# Generate with: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# SUPABASE_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Optional sign-in allowlist. Supabase sign-up is open by default, so without
# this anyone who can reach the instance can create an account.
# ALLOWED_EMAILS=me@example.com,partner@example.com
